        <p class="profile-detail-region">Life · Egypt &amp; North Africa</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Akhenaten</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 1363 BCE – c. 1335 BCE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Born</span><span class="info-stat-val">Probably before his father's 25th regnal year, c. 1363–1361 BCE (Donald Redford's dating; c. 1373 BCE elsewhere), place uncertain (Malkata or Memphis); a younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye</a>, of the 18th Dynasty</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Died</span><span class="info-stat-val">In his 17th regnal year, c. 1335 BCE, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> (Amarna); first buried in a tomb in the Royal Wadi, then most likely moved to tomb KV55 in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-the-kings">Valley of the Kings</a>, though the identification of the mummy found there remains controversial</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Who they were</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">Pharaoh</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, reigning c. 1353–1336 or 1351–1334 BCE, as Amenhotep IV and from his fifth regnal year as Akhenaten; husband of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a>, his Great Royal Wife</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Known for</span><span class="info-stat-val">promoting the worship of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a>); changing his name from Amenhotep IV; founding the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> (Amarna); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a>; the naturalistic Amarna art; the later erasure of his name and monuments; the debate over whether Atenism was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a></span></div>
        </div>
