    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born Amenhotep IV, a younger son who became heir after his brother Thutmose died, he came to the throne c. 1353 or 1351 BCE and in his fifth year took the name Akhenaten, 'Effective for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>'.</li><li>He founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> (Amarna), disbanded the priesthoods of the other gods, diverted their income to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> and, from about his ninth year, ordered the defacing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>'s temples throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</li><li>After his death his successors dismantled his monuments, destroyed his statues and excluded him from the lists of kings, calling him 'the enemy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>'; he was rediscovered only with the late 19th-century finds at Amarna.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> was built so quickly because its builders used small standardised blocks called talatats, about 27 by 27 by 54 cm; when archaeologists later dismantled the infill of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>'s pylons, some 36,000 decorated blocks from his Theban <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> temples emerged.</p></aside>
    </section>

