    <section class="rise-fall" id="timeline" aria-labelledby="timeline-title">
      <h2 class="profile-section-title" id="timeline-title">Timeline</h2>
      <ol class="rf-events"><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1363 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Amenhotep IV is born, according to Donald Redford's dating, a younger son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1353 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">He takes the throne as Amenhotep IV, in 1353 or 1351 BCE, most likely crowned at Thebes, and begins temples to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a> and elsewhere</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1352 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In his second regnal year he gives a speech, preserved on a pylon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>, saying that the gods had ceased their appearances except the god who gave birth to himself</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1349 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In his fifth regnal year he changes his name to Akhenaten and decrees a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>, now called Amarna</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1345 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In about his ninth regnal year he declares the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> the only worshipable god and orders the defacing of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>'s temples throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1342 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">In his twelfth regnal year the royal family receives tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1335 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Akhenaten dies and is buried in a tomb in the Royal Wadi east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a></span></li><li><span class="rf-year">c. 1332 BCE</span><span class="rf-text">Tutankhaten changes his name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> and abandons <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> returns gradually to its traditional religion</span></li></ol>
    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="early">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Early Life</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii.jpg" alt="Amenhotep III" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Colossal_Amenhotep_III_British_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Photograph by A. Parrot; sculptor unknown, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Akhenaten was born Amenhotep IV, a younger son of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a> and his principal wife, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye</a>. He had an elder brother, crown prince Thutmose, who was recognised as heir, and four or five sisters: Sitamun, Henuttaneb, Iset, Nebetah and possibly Beketaten. Egyptologists know very little of his life as a prince. Donald Redford dates his birth before his father's 25th regnal year, c. 1363–1361 BCE, from the birth of his first daughter, who was likely born fairly early in his reign. The only mention of his name, as "the King's Son Amenhotep", was found on a wine docket at Amenhotep III's Malkata palace, where some historians suggested he was born; others contend he was born in Memphis.</p><p>Thutmose's early death, perhaps around Amenhotep III's 30th regnal year, made Amenhotep IV next in line. Historians have proposed several tutors, among them the scribes Heqareshu or Meryre II, the royal tutor Amenemotep and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Aperel; the only person known for certain to have served the prince is Parennefer, whose tomb mentions it. Cyril Aldred suggested that he might have been High Priest of Ptah in Memphis, as Thutmose had been, although no evidence for this has been found. Redford and James Hoffmeier note that the cult of Ra was so widespread in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> that he could have been influenced by solar worship wherever he grew up.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Akhenaten&#8221; cites where it covers Family, Early life, Early reign:</p><ul><li>Ronald Thomas Ridley, Akhenaten: A Historian's View (The American University in Cairo Press, 2019)</li><li>Aidan Dodson, Amarna Sunset: Nefertiti, Tutankhamun, Ay, Horemheb, and the Egyptian Counter-Reformation (The American University in Cairo Press, 2018)</li><li>James Karl Hoffmeier, Akhenaten and the Origins of Monotheism (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li><li>Cyril Aldred, Akhenaten, King of Egypt (Thames and Hudson, 1991)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Family in the encyclopedia</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III<span>Father</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/tiye">Tiye<span>Mother</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti<span>Married</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/tadukhipa">Tadukhipa<span>Married</span></a><a href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun<span>Child</span></a></p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="rise">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Rise</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karnak.jpg" alt="Karnak" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_de_Louxor_68.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">René Hourdry, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Scholars disagree about how he came to the throne. Eric Cline, Nicholas Reeves, Peter Dorman and others argue against a long coregency with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amenhotep-iii">Amenhotep III</a>, favouring none or one lasting at most two years, while Donald Redford, William Murnane, Alan Gardiner and Lawrence Berman reject any coregency. In 2014 archaeologists found both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a>' names in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luxor">Luxor</a> tomb of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Amenhotep-Huy, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s Ministry of Antiquities called conclusive evidence of at least eight years of shared power; other Egyptologists say it shows only that the tomb was begun under one king and finished under the other.</p><p>Amenhotep IV took the throne most likely in 1353 or 1351 BCE; estimates of his age at accession range from 10 to 23. His reign began in the established manner. Inscriptions show him worshipping several gods, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/high-priest-of-amun">High Priest of Amun</a> was still active in his fourth year, and he continued his father's building at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnak">Karnak</a>'s Precinct of Amun-Re. At the same time he built shrines to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> in cities including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bubastis">Bubastis</a>, Heliopolis, Memphis and Kawa, and a large temple complex, the Per Aten, at Karnak. Around his second or third year he held a Sed festival, a rite usually held about the thirtieth year of a reign. Egyptologists only speculate why; they believe he made offerings during it to the Aten alone. He married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a>, his Great Royal Wife, either shortly before or after taking the throne.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Akhenaten&#8221; cites where it covers Early reign:</p><ul><li>Ronald Thomas Ridley, Akhenaten: A Historian's View (The American University in Cairo Press, 2019)</li><li>Cyril Aldred, Akhenaten, King of Egypt (Thames and Hudson, 1991)</li><li>James Karl Hoffmeier, Akhenaten and the Origins of Monotheism (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li><li>Jocelyn Olive Gohary, Akhenaten's Sed-festival at Karnak (Kegan Paul International, 1992)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="deeds">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">What They Did</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amarna-letters.jpg" alt="Amarna letters" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Five_Amarna_letters_on_display_at_the_British_Museum,_LondonA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Osama Shukir Muhammed Amin FRCP(Glasg), CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>In his fifth regnal year Amenhotep IV took decisive steps to establish the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> as the sole god of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>: he disbanded the priesthoods of the other gods, diverted their income to the Aten and changed his name to Akhenaten, understood by Gertie Englund and Florence Friedman as "Effective for the Aten". About the same time he decreed a new capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>, on an uninhabited site on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> about halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a> and Memphis. Its boundary stelae survive. It was built quickly with small talatat blocks, and by his eighth year the royal family could live there; only his most loyal subjects followed. By his ninth year he declared the Aten the only worshipable god, ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>'s temples defaced throughout Egypt, and banned images other than a rayed solar disc.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten</a>, found in the tomb of Ay, may have been composed by Akhenaten himself. It calls the Aten a sole god and creator and names the king as the only one who knows him. In foreign affairs the 382 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amarna-letters">Amarna letters</a> show a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> who kept control of the core of Egypt's Near Eastern empire, while avoiding conflict with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a>. Only one campaign is known for certain, against Nubian nomads in his second or twelfth year; Wolfgang Helck called it a small police operation and Alan Schulman a war of major proportions.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Akhenaten&#8221; cites where it covers Coregency with Amenhotep III, Early reign, Name change, Founding Amarna:</p><ul><li>Ronald Thomas Ridley, Akhenaten: A Historian's View (The American University in Cairo Press, 2019)</li><li>Cyril Aldred, Akhenaten, King of Egypt (Thames and Hudson, 1991)</li><li>William Lambert Moran (ed.), The Amarna Letters (The Johns Hopkins University Press, 1992)</li><li>Aidan Dodson, Amarna Sunrise: Egypt from the Golden Age to Age of Heresy (The American University in Cairo Press, 2014)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="opponents">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Opponents &amp; Critics</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rib-hadda.jpg" alt="Rib-Hadda" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablet_official_letter-AO_7093-P5280232-gradient.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Rama, CC BY-SA 3.0 fr</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The boundary stela that explained the move to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> is damaged, but its surviving parts speak of something worse than the king had heard before and of speech offensive to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a>. Egyptologists believe this refers to conflict with the priesthood and followers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>; Donald Redford suggested he was trying to break with them. Evidence on how far he forced his reforms is mixed. Some courtiers kept names such as Ahmose, and a sculptor at Amarna was called Thutmose, after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thoth">Thoth</a>; amulets of Bes, Taweret and the eye of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horus">Horus</a> were openly worn there. Yet ordinary residents also chiselled out references to Amun on small personal items, perhaps for fear of being accused of Amunist sympathies. His policies may have been fairly tolerant until some point toward the end of the reign.</p><p>His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rib-hadda">Rib-Hadda</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> wrote 60 letters pleading for help against Amurru, and Akhenaten did not send an army; Rib-Hadda was exiled and almost certainly executed. Early Egyptologists such as Henry Hall and James Henry Breasted read the letters as showing a pacifist who neglected foreign policy, a view discounted by the 21st century. Jacobus van Dijk and Jan Assmann hold that the Amarna period began a gradual decline in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a>'s standing, because the reforms subverted how ordinary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptians">Egyptians</a> related to their gods and king.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="last">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Last Years</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nefertiti.jpg" alt="Nefertiti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nofretete_Neues_Museum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Philip Pikart, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The last five years of the reign are poorly attested. The year 12 reception, at which the royal family received tribute from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/land-of-punt">Punt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> islands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, is regarded by Aidan Dodson as the zenith of the reign. Donald Redford and others proposed that an epidemic, most likely plague, struck <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> afterwards; it might account for the deaths of his daughters Meketaten, Neferneferure and Setepenre. An inscription found in 2012 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deir-el-bersha">Deir el-Bersha</a>, dated to year 16, shows that Akhenaten and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nefertiti">Nefertiti</a> were still a royal couple a year before his death. He may have ruled with Smenkhkare, who could have been his coregent by year 13 or 14, and later with Nefertiti.</p><p>Akhenaten died in his 17th year and was first buried in a tomb in the Royal Wadi east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>, which he had ordered on a boundary stela. His mummy was removed after Tutankhaten left Akhetaten and was most likely moved to tomb KV55 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, later desecrated. Whether the mummy found there in 1907 is his remains controversial: a team led by Zahi Hawass in 2010 called it the father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> and thus most probably Akhenaten, but others, among them Juan Belmonte, consider it more likely to be Smenkhkare. Smenkhkare may have ruled briefly alone; some think Nefertiti or Meritaten then ruled as Neferneferuaten for about two years, followed by Tutankhaten, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Ay administering the country.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Akhenaten&#8221; cites where it covers Later years, Death and burial, Possible illness:</p><ul><li>Ronald Thomas Ridley, Akhenaten: A Historian's View (The American University in Cairo Press, 2019)</li><li>Aidan Dodson, Amarna Sunset: Nefertiti, Tutankhamun, Ay, Horemheb, and the Egyptian Counter-Reformation (The American University in Cairo Press, 2009)</li><li>Aidan Dodson, Amarna Sunset: Nefertiti, Tutankhamun, Ay, Horemheb, and the Egyptian Counter-Reformation (The American University in Cairo Press, 2018)</li><li>Dominic Montserrat, Akhenaten: History, Fantasy and Ancient Egypt (Routledge, 2003)</li></ul></details>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="legacy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Legacy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tutankhamun.jpg" alt="Tutankhamun" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tutanchamun_Maske.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">MykReeve, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>After his death the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> cult fell out of favour, at first gradually and then decisively. Tutankhaten changed his name to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tutankhamun">Tutankhamun</a> in the second year of his reign, c. 1332 BCE, and abandoned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horemheb">Horemheb</a>, the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaoh</a> after Akhenaten who was not related to his family, temples to the Aten were destroyed and their blocks reused, including for temples of the restored <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amun">Amun</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seti-i">Seti I</a> restored Amun's name on inscriptions and had Akhenaten, Smenkhkare, Neferneferuaten, Tutankhamun and Ay excised from the lists of pharaohs. The Ramessides gradually destroyed Akhetaten. Later records call him "the enemy of Akhetaten" or "that criminal". The reforms also left a mark on language: texts of the Amarna period included more vernacular forms, which remained common in official texts from the 19th Dynasty.</p><p>Akhenaten was all but lost to history until the late 19th-century discovery of Amarna. Whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a> is disputed: some Egyptologists call it monolatrous, syncretistic or henotheistic, since he did not deny other gods. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmund-freud">Sigmund Freud</a> argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> had been an Atenist priest; in 1997 Redford concluded there is little or no evidence that Akhenaten was a progenitor of the monotheism of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a>, which had its own development beginning more than half a millennium after his death. Scholars such as Brian Fagan and Robert Alter have since reopened the debate. He has been described from "the first individual in history" to "heretic" and "possibly insane". The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/druze">Druze</a> faith recognises him as a prophet.</p></div><details class="sec-src"><summary>Sources for this section</summary><p class="sec-src-n">Works that Wikipedia&#8217;s article &#8220;Akhenaten&#8221; cites where it covers Legacy:</p><ul><li>Richard H. Wilkinson, The Complete Gods and Goddesses of Ancient Egypt (Thames &amp; Hudson, 2003)</li><li>James Karl Hoffmeier, Akhenaten and the Origins of Monotheism (Oxford University Press, 2015)</li><li>Ronald Thomas Ridley, Akhenaten: A Historian's View (The American University in Cairo Press, 2019)</li><li>Nicholas Reeves, Akhenaten: Egypt's False Prophet (Thames &amp; Hudson, 2019)</li></ul></details><div class="profile-inv"><p class="profile-inv-k">Songs in history</p><p class="profile-inv-l"><a href="/songs/harpers-songs">Harper's Songs<span>c. 1900 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/great-hymn-to-the-aten">Great Hymn to the Aten<span>c. 1350 BCE</span></a><a href="/songs/psalm-104">Psalm 104<span>date unknown</span></a></p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>Akhenaten (born Amenhotep IV, reigned c. 1353–1336 or 1351–1334 BCE) elevated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aten">Aten</a> above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s other gods, founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhetaten">Akhetaten</a> (Amarna) and changed his name in his fifth year. His successors erased his monuments and name; scholars still dispute whether <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atenism">Atenism</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a>.</em></p>

